          "source_text_sha256": "434664013307147b87d722d7ea22fd68f1022f7da5dd8bc3fab1e06004cc6efd",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Summary: Observation of several colored lights at the horizon: certain observation of nocturnal lighting signaling from two distinct groups of wind turbines and a flare from an Iridium satellite.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "63b6642e8a0733aaadcc00971276723c798d094f1d1c01d2511fe4c4dc4b4609"
        },
        {
          "end_ms": null,
          "error": null,
          "field": "description",
          "page_id": "page-feea47dd4e6b282aea9d387e",
          "qa_findings": [],
          "review_status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "segment_id": "seg-e06b34f9942539448e5d0c43",
          "segment_index": 13,
          "source_segment_id": "seg-e06b34f9942539448e5d0c43",
          "source_text_sha256": "ab267ec46a6b3d4e9491227ef98ec16a5453222d80b1bef8a8b2f31c7bf52ebd",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Description: On September 21, 2014, around 9 PM, a driver and their passengers were intrigued by an unknown light phenomenon in the sky. Only one testimony was collected. Based on the objective elements gathered (see investigation report), namely: - the color, regular flickering, and immobility, identical to the characteristics of the regulated nighttime lighting of wind turbines. - excellent visibility allowing observation from a great distance. - azimuth and elevation measurements allowing visibility of wind turbine lights from successive witness positions. - low angular height. - the illusion of movement amplified by the absence of references, preventing the witness from accurately assessing the azimuths - and, on the other hand: - Calsky data confirming the existence of a powerful -7.0 magnitude reflection on an Iridium satellite, visible from position 1 of the witnesses, around the time of observation - the rapid disappearance, consistent with the short visibility of flares.\n\nThe GEIPAN concludes that the case initially concerns the simultaneous observation towards the West of the nighttime lighting of a first group of wind turbines and a flare produced by an Iridium satellite, and subsequently, towards the southwest, of the nighttime lighting of a second group of wind turbines. This case is classified as \"A\" for a certain observation of the nighttime lighting of two distinct groups of wind turbines and an Iridium flare.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "51dc07bea6a9a7fe191e409c82e4f37a6d278ffe26e0edaac29ee1916dc4de51"
        }
      ],
